ther  difficult metabolites for t cells to  cope with you know lactate reactive  oxygen species etc  in that context hypoxia reduction alone  may be insufficient to metabolically  restore t-cell function  on the other hand in the responders we  saw evidence of underlying particularly  innate inflammatory responses interferon  alpha inflammatory response genes et  cetera stat5 signaling  as well as some early signs of adaptive  immunity in terms of interfering gamma  but not the kind of adaptive immune  signature that's been associated with  for instance response to ctla4 a  response to pd1  this is quite distinct and i think what  this represents is  when you have some underlying innate  inflammation but the presence of hypoxia  is preventing  that from actually nucleating a real  adaptive immune response that could be  mobilized against the tumor  the drug reducing that hypoxia now  allows this underlying inflammation to  manifest into better adaptive immune  filtration and anti-tumor immunity  so we do have a phase two trial planned  um  approximately 50  pancreatic prostate and hpv negative  head and neck patients  this is kind of an exciting trial for us  in addition to ctla 4 blockade much like  you saw the preclinical data these  patients will also receive pd1 blockade  again we feel like we probably under  treated them a little in the first trial  so they'll be getting  they'll be getting evophosphamide for  the first three cycles of their  immunotherapy as well so a little more  chance for as you know for us to sort of  plateau the amount of hypoxia reduction  and we'll be doing some hypoxia imaging  of these patients using the 18f fosa pet  tracer so for us this is a really  exciting one uh we're hoping it'll open  around the november or december of this  year  now where else have we seen this you  know high oxford signature associated  with checkpoint blockade resistance and  i'll say again you know greg delgoff has  published on this phenomenon as well so  um  we were lucky enough to be involved with  another trial uh you know sort of  stemming from some some work we had done  or very similar to some work we had done  pre-clinically looking at hpv  vaccination and checkpoint blockade  where bonnie glsen led a trial at md  anderson looking at um  pd1 blockade with the volume ab combined  with the iso 101 hpv vaccine  and and what we saw if you see on the  right panel was you know as opposed to  the baseline let's say you know 10 to 20  percent more like 13  response rate to pd-1 blockade in these  patients  uh the response rate in this trial was  more like 36 so really you about tripled  the response compared to pd1 alone  we'll go into that you know vaccination  gives us a higher again substrate of t  cells for that pd-1 blockade to work  with  they work together very well hpv e6 and  e7 are ideal antigens because they're  virally derived essential for  maintenance of the transformed phenotype  the tumor can't just stop expressing  them  and so we're really happy to see this  result  but even in this trial when we looked at  what are the gene sets associated with  lack of response to this you know really  therapeutically potent combination of  pd-1 blockade and  tumor antigen vaccination  oxfoss again number one gene set and so  we were seeing this over and over  so we went back to the lab and we looked  at some other data we had from a mouse  model and that data showed us that  um you know there are two origins of  hypoxia right in tumors so one that  we've talked about and the one that we  addressed with th 302  disordered vasculature leads to lack of  oxygen supply  which you know leads to hypoxia but the  other side of the equation is hyper  oxygen consumption so elevated oxford  metabolism  by tumors also promotes hypoxia by you  know basically eating all of the oxygen  that's available in the microenvironment  acting together these two mechanisms  establish a truly  um  deep hypoxia that t cells you know have  again a huge struggle to survive and  function within  and so you know we had done a prior uh  experiment where we took a  melanoma tumor so b16 melanoma and mice  treated them with both ctla4 pd1 and  pdl1 blockade in the context of a  vaccine the data i showed you in the  very first slide i showed you  which is about 70 or 80 curative  we took the 20 that didn't get cured put  them back into new mice treated them  again and we kept repeating that until  we evolved the tumor line that was  completely refractory to the original  immunotherapy that cured 80 of them and  then we asked well how did that  you know how did these b16 melanomas  that were so sensitive to triple  checkpoint blockade become completely  resistant  and i'll be honest with you we expected  to find they had upregulated new novel  checkpoints or they were making a ton of  immunosuppressive cytokine but the truth  is the main changes were all metabolic  and what you could see is that when we  looked on the right panel  at this immune resistant melanoma we  found that it had highly upregulated  oxfos  there was enhanced uh adaptation to  hypoxia so this gene set  um labeled as hypoxia it's actually a  gene set associated with adaptation of  hypoxic stress  as well as adaptation to oxidative  stress so when you up regulate oxfos  metabolism you're going to be creating  as a byproduct a lot more reactive  oxygen species um it's the up regulation  the coincident upregulation of the  oxidative stress machinery that allows  tumors to actually you know sort of  prosper in the face of this high ross  level  on the other hand if instead we looked  in the micro environment so this was a  model where we could separate the tumor  cells from the surrounding stroma  in the surrounding stroma you know as  the tumors sort of flourished and  increased their ox fats and oxygen  consumption the stroma was deprived of  oxford's metabolism basically the tumor  is hyper consuming all the oxygen  there's nothing left for the stroma  um hypoxic adaptation  or or adaptation of toxic stress was  exactly inverted from the tumor so so  these stroma were experiencing um high  signatures of being exposed to high  levels of oxidative stress so think of  it as a  lack of adaptation to hypoxic stress and  of course cd8 cell effector function  associated genes bottomed out right so  you're you're the immune component of  this stroma that can no longer prosecute  oxford's metabolism and is now being  exposed to intense hypoxic and oxidative  stress  is non-functional  and so we thought of this you know  mechanistic study in light of finding  this  hypox gene set associated with you know  lack of response in our hypoxia  immunotherapy trial lack of response in  our  pd-1 vaccine trial  and so we went back to the lab and said  well can we also inhibit oxfos what  happens if we block that end of the  equation at the same time we're trying  to remodel vasculature and restore  oxygenation with h3o2  now i will say that you know there  there's  there's some amount of lack of  selectivity of oxfast inhibitors and t  cells do need some amount of loss  metabolism particularly when they're  activating and expanding initially  however what we saw with one particular  oxfast inhibitor shown here which is the  ix-10759 inhibitor was that in the  context of checkpoint blockades so ctla4  and pd1 blockade we had a much higher  density of t cells in red here uh  when we combined ctla4 and pd1 with  1.759 and what you can see sort of in  the bar graph is that t cells alone is  under the vehicle plus anti-ctla for  anti-pd1 um there's pretty much nothing  there at day 12 where there's this huge  bar it's a very significant both cd3  under normoxia and cv300 hypoxia with a  combination with oxford inhibitor so  that was telling us t-cell density in  the tram c2 tumors was improved in the  context of immunotherapy and oxford  inhibition  so that led us to investigate a little  further you know how far we could push  this pathway and we looked at three  oxfast inhibitors so these are three  that are sort of clinically relevant uh  iax10759 which i showed you im156 from  immunomet and metformin which metformin  can inhibit oxfords at high  concentrations it's a little bit of a  dirty drug because as you all know it  also does many other things however this  is where we focused our efforts  and that was just seahorse assays  showing you that they they all really do  suppress oxford's metabolism  what we found we looked at 10759 or 156  was that when we use them in combination  with th 302 which is the pink line in  the graphs on on the left  you know they they had a very  significant additive benefit maybe even  synergistic in in suppressing tram c2  tumor growth so even without putting  immunotherapy on top  just combining th 302 and oxford's  inhibition um again which is for us  getting to both the sort of push pull of  this origins of hypoxia  had a very strong therapeutic benefit  now in the right panels you see we did  combinations with ctla4 and pd1 blockade  and here in the combination setting the  tumors were you know essentially wiped  out so we really saw you know some very  encouraging data  unfortunately you know the tumors were  really wiped out uh both by the  combinations of the oxfox inhibitor  alone and checkpoint blockade as well as  the triple combination with ctla4 pd1  and th3o2 so we have follow-on studies  uh in a harder to treat tumor model  where we're trying to to you know really  get a cleaner idea of how much additive  or synergistic benefit there is where  we're not really just curing everything  with all the combinations however we  found this really encouraging and it's  something we're trying to develop as a  way to address this resistance that  we're seeing in the clinic  um when we look mechanistically i won't  go into too much detail but you can see  that you know when we combine th 302 and  oxfos inhibitor 107.59  we see high-level cd8 ki-67  proliferation in the tumor high-level  grants ib production  cd4-t effector proliferation is high  a lot of this is driven by the ctla4 pd1  alone but we definitely see  some benefit in the triple  therapeutically  where where is that coming from well one  place we see it is in down regulation of  arginase production by tumor associated  macrophages  again i mentioned the benefit of hypoxia  to them in terms of uh you know m2  polarization and arginine subregulation  there's also a benefit of oxford's  metabolism  and and mhc2 upregulation is is really  high in the context of arginase down  regulation something we see associated  with really like an m1 phenotypic switch  so we're we're still developing this  data the last pieces aren't published  but um  but you know we're seeing a really  encouraging trend that if we can block  tumor oxfords uh or diminish it in the  context of restoring oxygenation with th  302  we create a much more favorable  metabolic environment for antitumor  immunity particularly driven by t cells  so that's it i want to thank in  particular the people in my lab that did  this work  um  priya is the postdoc who's really led  all of this she's fantastic um arthur  krithika and you know prior um megan and  meet on ashvin did some of that work i  told you about with the the resistant uh  melanoma line and  you know seeing oxfords and hypoxic  stress uh upregulation  so um again also thank david hong and  immunogenesis and molecular templates  for their work on the clinical trial and  you know thanks again to lab roots for  giving me this opportunity to present  and i hope you all enjoyed the talk
 

